      "stream_token_id_count": 512,
      "text": "\n\nBelow is a structured comparison of the two most common ways to serve a single large model on a 4\u2011GPU node:\n\n- Option A: Tensor parallel (TP) across 4 GPUs (one logical replica)\n- Option B: 4 independent 1\u2011GPU replicas behind a router (data parallel / model parallel by replication)\n\nI\u2019ll assume a typical local inference service (e.g., vLLM, TGI, SGLang, or a custom FastAPI + torch backend) and a model that either fits on one GPU (B) or does not (A).\n\n1) Latency\n\n- TP (4 GPUs)\n  - Time to first token (TTFT): Lower for large models because the forward pass is split across GPUs; each layer\u2019s compute is parallelized.\n  - Inter-token latency (ITL): Can be slightly higher per token due to all-reduce/all-gather communication after each layer. On NVLink this is small; on PCIe it can dominate.\n  - Overall: Best for low-latency, single-request, large-model serving.\n- 4\u00d71\u2011GPU replicas\n  - TTFT: Higher if the model is large and must be computed on a single GPU (more FLOPs per GPU, no layer parallelism).\n  - ITL: Lower per token if the model fits comfortably on one GPU and batch size is small (no cross-GPU sync).\n  - Overall: Good for small/medium models where per-GPU compute is sufficient; latency is dominated by single-GPU compute.\n\n2) Throughput\n\n- TP (4 GPUs)\n  - Throughput is limited by the slowest GPU and by communication overhead.\n  - Batching helps, but TP does not multiply throughput linearly; you get one faster replica.\n  - Best when you need low latency and the model is too big for one GPU.\n- 4\u00d71\u2011GPU replicas\n  - Throughput scales nearly linearly with the number of replicas (up to memory and PCIe limits).\n  - The router can load-balance across replicas, so you can serve 4\u00d7 the concurrent requests (if each replica can handle its share).\n  - Best for high-throughput, latency-tolerant workloads with models that fit on one GPU.\n\n3) PCIe Traffic\n\n- TP (4 GPUs)\n  - Heavy PCIe traffic: every layer requires all-reduce/all-gather across GPUs.\n ",
      "text_preview": "\n\nBelow is a structured comparison of the two most common ways to serve a single large model on a 4\u2011GPU node:\n\n- Option A: Tensor parallel (TP) across 4 GPUs (one logical replica)\n- Option B: 4 independent 1\u2011GPU replicas behind a router (data parallel / model parallel by replication)\n\nI\u2019ll assume a typical local infere",
